el Pellegrini to sigh forlornly about his new forward=E2=80=99s mistakes. B=
ut the beauty of Parrott these days is his refusal to sulk. Coming off the =
bench in the 68th minute against Madrid, he reacted quickest to a Thibaut C=
ourtois parry, shoving the ball into the net from close range. No stepovers=
, no 30-yard curlers, just pure instinct. =E2=80=9CThat=E2=80=99s a striker=
=E2=80=99s job,=E2=80=9D Parrott squawked. =E2=80=9CIn a game, I might do t=
his 10 times, and it might not happen. So, it=E2=80=99s a mentality thing t=
o just keep on going in there, hoping it comes. Today, it did.=E2=80=9D</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">It also happened to come against Mourinho.=
 The same Mourinho who had decided Parrott was not quite ready was now watc=
hing the 24-year-old end his perfect start to his second spell at Madrid. F=
ootball does enjoy a punchline. And Parrott had another one ready for Lille=
, pouncing on some poor defending by Nathan Ngoy to fire a low effort into =
the far corner in a thrilling 3-2 win. Four appearances, two goals, two win=
ners, Madrid slain, and a Bigger Cup debut goal logged. Meanwhile, Tottenha=
m sit in the Premier League=E2=80=99s bottom three still waiting for their =
first league goal. There is, naturally, a danger in getting carried away. T=
wo goals do not suddenly mean Spurs made the biggest transfer blunder since=
 selling Gareth Bale. Parrott is 24 now, not the teenager Mourinho was deal=
ing with, and his development has been anything but straightforward. But th=
at is what makes his current run so enjoyable: he has had to take the sceni=
c route.</p>
